{% set local_problem %}
<p>Scottsboro's tree coverage is beautiful until you own a house here. Pine needles year-round. Oak and hickory leaves every fall. Tupelo and sweetgum adding their debris from spring through autumn. Your gutters catch all of it.</p>

<p>For full-time residents, that means 3-4 cleanings per year at $150-200 each, or $450-800 annually in maintenance. Annoying, but manageable.</p>

<p>For vacation homeowners near <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> or along <a href="/recent-projects/">Tupelo Pike</a>, the math is different. You're not there to notice when gutters fill up. Debris accumulates for months. In Scottsboro's humidity, that debris doesn't just sit there — it absorbs moisture, compacts, and turns into a dense sludge we call "gutter concrete." It holds water against the metal. It weighs down the gutters. It creates exactly the conditions that cause overflow and fascia rot.</p>

<p>We cleaned a set of gutters in <a href="/recent-projects/">Lake Guntersville Shores</a> that had been neglected for two years. The debris was so compacted we had to chisel it out. Literally chisel — scraping didn't work. The homeowner's $75 cleaning estimate turned into $350 of labor because of what the humidity had done to normal leaf debris.</p>

<p>Gutter guards prevent that cycle entirely. Debris lands on top of the guard, dries out, and blows or washes off. Your gutters stay clear and functional whether you're checking them weekly or visiting twice a year.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are covers that fit over or into your existing gutters. They let water through while blocking debris. Sounds simple, but execution matters — especially in humid environments.</p>

<p><strong>Foam guards</strong> — sponge-like inserts that fill the gutter. Cheap and easy to install. Also terrible near the lake. The foam absorbs humidity, stays wet, and breaks down within 18-24 months. We've pulled out foam guards that turned to mush. Don't waste your money.</p>

<p><strong>Screen guards</strong> — mesh or perforated covers over the gutter opening. Better than foam, but fine debris (pine needles, seed pods, roof grit) falls through and accumulates anyway. In Scottsboro's humidity, that fine debris becomes the same sludge problem, just slower.</p>

<p><strong>Micro-mesh guards</strong> — surgical-steel or aluminum mesh with openings around 50-100 microns. Blocks everything including pine needles and shingle grit. Water passes through via surface tension and capillary action. More expensive, but actually works long-term in humid environments. This is what we install on most <a href="/recent-projects/">Scottsboro lake properties</a>.</p>

<p><strong>Reverse-curve/solid top guards</strong> — water follows the curved surface into a slot while debris falls off the edge. Works well for large leaves, less effective for pine needles which can stick to the curve. Mixed results in our experience.</p>

<p>For Scottsboro's mix of pine and hardwood debris plus the humidity factor, micro-mesh is usually the right answer. It costs more upfront. It works when cheaper options don't.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed gutter guards throughout Jackson County for over 20 years. We've seen what works in Scottsboro's humidity and what fails. (Foam fails. Every time.) We recommend based on your actual situation — tree types, roof pitch, maintenance tolerance — not based on what has the highest margin.</p>

